  "summary_text": "Kyle, Port Washington is performing well year-over-year\u2014up 36% in revenue and 35% in appointments\u2014and MTD is tracking essentially on plan once you adjust for calendar mix (within 1.1% of DOW-adjusted expectation). Yesterday's 19% revenue dip is real but traceable: you ran 5 groomers at $469/groomer against a trailing-30-day baseline of $502/groomer, which is a 7% productivity miss, not a volume collapse. Add-on attachment cratered from 57% to 42% and retail dropped 30%\u2014these are operational signals that warrant a front-of-house review. Hicksville is the concern. Despite being up 11.4% DOW-adjusted MTD, yesterday you ran 5 groomers at $372/groomer against a $403 baseline, and year-to-date productivity ($370/groomer-day) is 18% below Port Washington ($493). Hicksville's groomer pool is undershooting by structural design or capability; you're also carrying zero bather hours there while Port is running 8. Retail spiked yesterday (up 181%), but that's noise\u2014grooming attach is what matters, and it's flagging soft. Both stores show a pattern: add-on discipline is slipping. Glen Cove, your smallest location, is the only store firing on all cylinders\u2014up 52% WTD, $360/groomer yesterday against a $237 baseline\u2014but you've scheduled zero groomers for the next two days and zero for the next seven, which means that momentum stops cold.\n\nYour one bright spot: year-to-date growth across Port and Hicksville combined is 32% revenue and 33% appointments. That's real.\n\nYour one watch: Hicksville productivity and coverage need a structural reset, and you're walking away from Glen Cove's momentum with no scheduling next week.\n\nPort Washington today: 4 groomers, 8.0h bathers. Tomorrow: 1 groomer, 15.0h bathers. Next 7 days: 36 groomer-days total."
